[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "The five desirable properties identified by Curtis, Kellner, and Over (1992) are:",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "There are many process modeling tools and techniques, and researchers continue to work to determine which ones are most appropriate for a given situation. But there are some characteristics that are helpful, regardless of technique. Curtis, Kellner, and Over (1992) have identified five categories of desirable properties:",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet_intro",
    "segment": "Five desirable properties",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "1. Facilitates  human understanding and communication. The technique should be able to represent the process in a form that most customers and developers can understand, encouraging communication about the process and agreement on its form and improvements. The technique should include sufficient information to allow one or more people to actually perform the process.And the model or tool should form a basis for training.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "Facilitates human understanding and communication: the technique should be understandable to customers and developers, encourage communication, enable performance of the process, and form a basis for training.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "1. Facilitates  human understanding and communication. The technique should be able to represent the process in a form that most customers and developers can understand, encouraging communication about the process and agreement on its form and improvements. The technique should include sufficient information to allow one or more people to actually perform the process.And the model or tool should form a basis for training.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "Supports process improvement: identify essential components, enable reuse, compare alternatives, and estimate impact of changes before implementation; aid in tool selection, organizational learning, and evolution of the process.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "2. Supports process improvement. The technique should identify the essential components of a development or maintenance process. It should allow reuse of processes or subprocesses on subsequent projects, compare alternatives, and estimate the impact of changes before the process is actually put into practice. Similarly, the technique should assist in selecting tools and techniques for the process, in encouraging organizational learning, and in supporting continuing evolution of the process.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "Supports process management: enable project-specific reasoning about attributes, and support planning, forecasting, monitoring, managing, and measuring key process characteristics.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "3. Supports  process  management. The  technique  should  allow  the  process  to  be project-specific. Then, developers and customers should be able to reason about attributes of software creation or evolution. The technique should also support planning and forecasting, monitoring and managing the process, and measuring key process characteristics.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "Provides automated guidance: define parts of the environment, provide guidance and suggestions, and retain reusable representations for later use.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "4. Provides automated guidance in performing the process. The technique should define all or part of the software development environment, provide guidance and suggestions, and retain reusable process representations for later use.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  },
  {
    "role": "bullet",
    "segment": "Supports automated process execution: automate all or part of the process, support cooperative work, capture measurement data, and enforce rules to ensure process integrity.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "5. Supports automated process execution. The technique should automate all or part of  the  process, support  cooperative work, capture relevant measurement data, and enforce rules to ensure process integrity.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

