[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Graham (1996a) summarizes the differences between object-oriented and traditional testing in two ways. First, she notes which aspects of object orientation make testing easier and which make it harder.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Graham (1996a) summarizes the differences between object-oriented and traditional testing in two ways. First, she notes which aspects of object orientation make testing easier and which make it harder.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "For example, objects tend to be small, and the complexity that might ordinarily reside in the component is often pushed instead toward the interfaces among components. This difference means that unit testing is less difficult, but integration testing must be much more extensive.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "For example, objects tend to be small, and the",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "complexity  that  might  ordinarily  reside  in  the  component  is  often  pushed  instead  toward  the interfaces among components.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "This difference means that unit testing is less difficult, but integration testing must be much more extensive.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "As we have seen, encapsulation is often considered a positive attribute of object-oriented design, but it also requires more extensive integration testing.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "as we have seen, encapsulation is often considered a positive attribute of object-oriented design, but it also requires more extensive integration testing.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Similarly, inheritance introduces the need for more testing. An inherited function needs additional testing if it is redefined, if it has a specific behavior in a derived class, or if other functions in that class are supposed to be consistent.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Similarly, inheritance introduces the need for more testing.An inherited function needs additional testing if",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "- it is redefined",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "- it has a specific behavior in a derived class",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "- other functions in that class are supposed to be consistent",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  }
]

[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "To satisfy statement testing and all-uses testing for a piece of code, you would design test cases that ensure every executable statement runs at least once and that for every variable definition, you exercise every use exactly as required by the all-uses strategy.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "To test code thoroughly, we can choose test cases using at least one of several approaches based on the data manipulated by the code:",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet_intro",
        "segment": "Test cases for statement testing and all-uses testing",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Statement testing: Every statement in the component is executed at least once in some test.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "1) Identify all statements in the code and create tests that execute each statement at least once. For control-flow statements (if, switch, loops), ensure at least one path executes the statement inside each branch across tests.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Statement testing: Every statement in the component is executed at least once in some test.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "2) For all-uses testing, identify every definition of each variable and ensure there is at least one test path that reaches every use of that definition.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "All-uses testing: The test set includes at least one path from every definition to every use that can be reached by that definition.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ],
        "type": null
    },
    {
        "role": "bullet",
        "segment": "3) Combine both goals by crafting tests that execute every statement while also ensuring that, for each variable definition, all subsequent uses are covered along some test path.",
